Spec: Changing pointer-masking PMLEN without TLB invalidation creates stale GPA-to-SPA mappings under two-stage translation, and HLVX.* instruction-fetch emulation must never apply pointer masking to prevent fetching from attacker-controlled aliased addresses.
  [BUG] lib/sbi/sbi_fwft.c:237-263  fwft_set_pmlen
      Changes menvcfg.PMM (PMLEN) via csr_read_clear+csr_set at lines 255-256 without any subsequent SFENCE.VMA or HFENCE.GVMA. Changing PMLEN alters virtual-address interpretation for all M-mode accesses including MPRV-emulated loads/stores. Stale TLB entries keyed on the old masking width can hit wrong physical pages — this is the exact TLB-aliasing vulnerability from the spec. The function at lines 170-182 (fwft_set_adue) in the same file, which modifies the same CSR (menvcfg), correctly calls __sbi_sfence_vma_all() and __sbi_hfence_gvma_all() after its write. The fix is to add those same two fence calls after the menvcfg.PMM write.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_fwft.c:170-182  fwft_set_adue
      Correct reference pattern: modifies menvcfg (ADUE bit) then calls __sbi_sfence_vma_all() and conditionally __sbi_hfence_gvma_all() to invalidate TLB entries that may have been cached under the old CSR state. This is the canonical pattern that fwft_set_pmlen should replicate.
  [BUG] lib/sbi/sbi_domain_context.c:130-146  switch_to_next_domain_context
      The TLB flush at line 140 (__sbi_sfence_vma_all()) executes AFTER the SATP swap (line 139) but BEFORE the senvcfg swap (line 144). If the target domain has a different senvcfg.PMM value, the new pointer-masking configuration takes effect without any TLB invalidation. S-mode TLB entries cached under the old PMM regime remain valid while the new PMM is active. Under H-extension with vsatp.MODE=Bare, this is the exact two-stage TLB-aliasing hazard described in the spec. The fence must be moved to after the senvcfg swap, or a second fence must be added after line 144.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hsm.c:435-446  __sbi_hsm_suspend_non_ret_restore
      Restores menvcfg (including PMM field) at line 441 via csr_write64 without issuing a TLB flush. On non-retentive suspend, TLBs are typically cold (power-gated), so this is usually safe. However, correctness depends on the unverified hardware assumption that non-retentive suspend completely and atomically clears all TLB and address-translation cache state. If any microarchitectural TLB state persists (e.g., in non-volatile storage or a shared L2 TLB), stale entries from pre-suspend could alias against the restored PMLEN. A defensive SFENCE.VMA after the menvcfg write would eliminate this dependency.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_unpriv.c:21-86  DEFINE_UNPRIVILEGED_LOAD_FUNCTION
      All unprivileged load/store emulation functions use MSTATUS_MPRV to perform accesses under the current menvcfg.PMM-controlled address-translation regime. These functions themselves do not change PMLEN and are not responsible for TLB coherence — they are consumers of whatever PMLEN is currently active. However, because fwft_set_pmlen (the sole PMLEN setter) omits TLB fencing, these MPRV-based accesses become a direct exploitation path: after a PMLEN change, the new masking width is applied against TLB entries cached under the old masking width. The vulnerability root is in fwft_set_pmlen, not here.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_trap_ldst.c:339-365  sbi_load_access_handler
